📊 Cashflow Dashboard – Power BI Project
1. Description

The Cashflow Dashboard is an interactive Power BI report designed to monitor, analyze, and visualize customer-level cashflow insights. It helps stakeholders understand the financial stability of customers by presenting loan-related inflows/outflows, business cash positions, and expense patterns in a structured and intuitive manner.
The dashboard enables faster and more accurate decision-making during loan disbursement and risk assessment.

2. Business Requirement

The requirement was to build a unified reporting system that displays complete financial data of each customer, including business income, expenses, and cashflow trends.
The goal was to create a single-page report that provides clear, real-time financial visibility for analysts, credit teams, and management.

3. What It Solves

Provides end-to-end cashflow analysis for every customer.

Eliminates manual calculations and fragmented Excel/Google Sheet reports.

Supports better loan approval decisions based on true financial capability.

Helps in identifying risk patterns and ensuring accuracy during loan disbursement.

Offers a clean, consolidated, and interactive report for quick insights.

4. Data Sources

SQL Server (primary customer & financial data)

Google Sheets (operational inputs / updated customer info)

SharePoint (uploaded financial statements / additional data files)

5. Key Features

🔹 Customer-level cash inflow & outflow trends

🔹 Monthly and yearly cashflow comparison

🔹 Income–expense breakdown

🔹 Net cash position indicator

🔹 Dynamic filters (customer, region, branch, time period)

🔹 Drill-through pages for detailed customer profile

🔹 Automated data refresh through Power BI Service

🔹 Clean, business-ready UI/UX for stakeholders

6. Tools & Technologies

Power BI Desktop

Power BI Service

SQL Server

Excel / Google Sheets

SharePoint

DAX for KPIs and measures

Power Query (M Query) for ETL processes

7. How to Use

Open the .pbix file in Power BI Desktop.

Update the data source connections (SQL Server / SharePoint / Google Sheet).

Refresh the report to load the latest data.

Use the slicers (Customer, Branch, Month, Product Type) to filter insights.

Publish to Power BI Service for automated refresh and sharing.

Share the dashboard with business users for real-time monitoring.
